.TH LUXAFOR-TRAY 1 "" "luxafor-status" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
luxafor-tray \- system tray icon showing the Luxafor status
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B luxafor-tray
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B luxafor-tray
shows a dot in the system tray with the color of the current status.
Its menu sets a status in one click, starts one of the timer shortcuts
(see
.B timer-shortcuts
in
.BR luxafor-toggle (1)),
opens
.BR luxafor-panel (1)
with
.B "More..."
for any other duration and the settings (also with a middle-click on the
icon), turns the light off, or quits. While a timer runs, the time left is
shown next to the dot and at the top of the menu, with an entry to cancel it.
.PP
It follows changes made by
.BR luxafor-toggle (1)
from anywhere (launcher, keyboard shortcut, terminal) and reloads the
configuration when the file is saved.
.PP
It watches for the light: when it is unplugged (laptop off its dock), the
dot becomes a dashed ring, the menu starts with
.BR "Light not connected" ,
and a notification says so. When it is plugged back in \(em and at login
\(em the light shows the current status again.
.PP
When the screen is locked, the light switches to the
.B lock-status
setting (off by default) and the status from before comes back when it is
unlocked, unless it was changed meanwhile. It follows the screensaver D-Bus
interfaces of Budgie, GNOME, KDE, Cinnamon and MATE, and logind's LockedHint.
.PP
While the status is one of
.BR dnd-statuses ,
desktop notifications are silenced through the desktop's own "do not
disturb" switch; the user's setting is put back afterwards and when the tray
quits.
.PP
It is started at login through
.IR /etc/xdg/autostart/luxafor-tray.desktop .
.PP
Only one tray runs per session: starting it again replaces the running one.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.cache/luxafor-tray.log
Errors met while running.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR luxafor-panel (1),
.BR luxafor-light (1),
.BR luxafor-toggle (1)
